WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.320
How does Hamiltonian path reduce

2
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:04.900
to TSP? We will construct

3
00:00:04.900 --> 00:00:07.340
a complete weighted graph, choose

4
00:00:07.340 --> 00:00:09.240
the correct threshold, and prove

5
00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.080
both directions instead of relying

6
00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:12.800
on one persuasive picture.

7
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:15.600
A Hamiltonian path visits every

8
00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:17.960
vertex exactly once without requiring

9
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:19.200
a return to its start.

10
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:21.860
A Hamiltonian tour additionally closes

11
00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.500
the route, and that extra

12
00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:24.960
closing edge is why the

13
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:26.580
threshold in this construction needs

14
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:26.960
care.

15
00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:29.940
Assume an undirected graph G

16
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:31.480
with at least three vertices,

17
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.380
and call its vertex count

18
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:35.400
n. Build a complete graph

19
00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.940
T on exactly the same

20
00:00:36.940 --> 00:00:39.220
vertices, so every missing original

21
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:40.860
connection now also has an

22
00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:41.240
edge.

23
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.540
Assign weight one to edges

24
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.460
already in G and weight

25
00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:46.900
two to the newly added

26
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:49.440
edges. Ask whether T has

27
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:51.200
a Hamiltonian tour of total

28
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:52.720
cost at most n plus

29
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.100
one.

30
00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.580
In the five-vertex slide graph,

31
00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.460
original edges are A B,

32
00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:59.380
A C, A D, B

33
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:00.800
E, C E and C

34
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:02.860
D. The route E, B,

35
00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:04.840
A, C, D visits every

36
00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:06.740
vertex once using four original

37
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.360
edges, so it is a

38
00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:10.000
Hamiltonian path in G.

39
00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.760
Close that path from D

40
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:13.920
back to E in the

41
00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:16.220
complete graph. The closing edge

42
00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:18.000
was absent from G and

43
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:20.280
therefore costs two, giving total

44
00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.860
four plus two equal to

45
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:23.540
six, which is n plus

46
00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:23.980
one.

47
00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.320
The forward proof is general:

48
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:29.200
a Hamiltonian path uses n

49
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.160
minus one original edges of

50
00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:33.260
cost one. Its closing edge

51
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.060
costs either one or two,

52
00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.260
so the resulting tour costs

53
00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:39.000
at most n plus one.

54
00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.980
For the reverse proof, suppose

55
00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.780
the transformed graph has a

56
00:01:43.780 --> 00:01:45.380
tour costing at most n

57
00:01:45.380 --> 00:01:47.860
plus one. A Hamiltonian tour

58
00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:50.040
has exactly n edges, each

59
00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:51.900
costing at least one, so

60
00:01:51.900 --> 00:01:52.820
there is room for at

61
00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:54.420
most one weight-two edge.

62
00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.460
If the tour includes a

63
00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:59.060
weight-two edge, delete that single

64
00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:01.000
edge. What remains is a

65
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.520
Hamiltonian path using only weight-one

66
00:02:03.520 --> 00:02:05.200
edges, all of which belong

67
00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.820
to the original graph G.

68
00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:09.160
If every tour edge has

69
00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.080
weight one, remove any one

70
00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.800
of them instead. Again the

71
00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:14.820
remaining route visits all vertices

72
00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.060
exactly once using original edges,

73
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:19.340
completing the reverse implication.

74
00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.500
The construction inspects vertex pairs

75
00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:24.140
and assigns their weights in

76
00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:25.580
big O of n squared

77
00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:27.160
time under a suitable graph

78
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.880
representation. Its output size is

79
00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:31.960
also polynomial, and the threshold

80
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:33.380
n plus one has a

81
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:34.720
short binary encoding.

82
00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.460
Using threshold n would demand

83
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:39.220
that every tour edge already

84
00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.820
existed in G. That would

85
00:02:40.820 --> 00:02:42.680
ask for an original Hamiltonian

86
00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:44.360
cycle and could reject a

87
00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:45.780
graph that has a Hamiltonian

88
00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:47.700
path but needs one missing

89
00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:48.980
edge to close it.

90
00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:50.820
Things to take note of:

91
00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.260
preserve the same vertices, distinguish

92
00:02:53.260 --> 00:02:55.240
original and added edges, and

93
00:02:55.240 --> 00:02:56.760
prove both directions with the

94
00:02:56.760 --> 00:02:58.780
exact threshold n plus one.

95
00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.760
One added closing edge is

96
00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.160
allowed, two are not; that

97
00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.660
simple count is the argument

98
00:03:04.660 --> 00:03:06.800
your diagram must actually explain,

99
00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:07.540
hmph.
